{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-942e081bbb1e4762","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking me to explain how to verify a reversible software installation in 24 numbered paragraphs. Each paragraph must contain exactly three complete sentences and a distinct concrete example. Topics to cover: installation, configuration, dependencies, permissions, service restart, interruption, backup, user data, rollback.\n\nThe message starts with \"Start with READY.\" — so my response needs to begin with \"READY\".\n\nThis appears to be a structured writing task. Let me write 24 numbered paragraphs, each with exactly 3 sentences and a distinct concrete example, covering the required topics.\n\nLet me plan the 24 paragraphs:\n1. Overall verification approach (planning)\n2. Pre-installation baseline snapshot\n3. Installation — package manager verification\n4. Installation — file manifest verification\n5. Configuration — file location verification\n6. Configuration — default vs modified settings\n7. Dependencies — declared dependency check\n8. Dependencies — version pinning\n9. Permissions — file permissions baseline\n10. Permissions — user/group ownership\n11. Service restart — clean restart test\n12. Service restart — startup-time behavior\n13. Interruption — mid-installation interruption test\n14. Interruption — network failure during installation\n15. Backup — system state backup before installation\n16. Backup — configuration backup\n17. User data — isolation between app data and program files\n18. User data — no user data modified by installation\n19. Rollback — package manager removal test\n20. Rollback — snapshot restore test\n21. Rollback — residual file cleanup\n22. Rollback — restore service state after rollback\n23. Post-rollback verification\n24. Documentation / repeatability\n\nEach paragraph needs exactly 3 complete sentences. Each paragraph needs a distinct concrete example. Let me write carefully and count the sentences.\n\nEach paragraph: exactly 3 sentences. I have to be careful that abbreviations with periods don't get interpreted as sentence boundaries — but \"complete sentences\" means grammatical sentences, so I just need to make sure there are clearly 3 sentences per paragraph. Avoid abbreviations like \"e.g.\" to prevent ambiguity.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n1. Verification begins by defining reversibility as a measurable contract before touching the system, because a claim of reversibility means nothing without a testable definition. Write down the exact end state that must be restored, including file paths, service states, and configuration values that should be identical before and after. For example, if you install nginx on Ubuntu 24.04, define that after rollback the /etc/nginx directory must not exist, the nginx user must be removed, and port 8080 must be free.\n\nThat's 3 sentences. Good.\n\n2. Take a machine-readable baseline of the system before installing anything, so that you have a ground truth to diff against later. The baseline should capture installed packages, listening ports, running services, and checksums of configuration directories that the software is likely to touch. For example, on a Fedora server, run rpm -qa > baseline-packages.txt, ss -tlnp > baseline-ports.txt, and sha256sum /etc/hosts to create three snapshot files you can compare against after rollback.\n\n3 sentences. Good.\n\n3. Verify the installation method itself is reversible by choosing a transactional package manager over a manual tarball extraction whenever possible. A package manager records the file manifest it writes, giving you an authoritative list of what to remove later, whereas a tarball leaves no such record. For example, installing htop with apt install htop records every path in dpkg's database, while extracting a htop tarball to /usr/local leaves files scattered that you must remember yourself.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n4. After installation, confirm that the package manager's recorded manifest matches what actually landed on disk, because partial installs break rollback assumptions. Compare the list of files the package claims to own against a filesystem diff of the pre-install snapshot. For example, after installing redis-server on Debian, run dpkg -L redis-server and diff that list against the delta produced by a find-based snapshot taken before and after, checking that no orphan files appeared in /etc/redis beyond those listed.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n5. Verify that configuration files are written to predictable, documented locations rather than scattered across the system, since scattered configs are the most common source of rollback residue. Check that the software respects standard paths like /etc for system config and stores its own state in its designated directory. For example, when installing Prometheus, confirm it keeps its main config in /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml and its TSDB data in /var/lib/prometheus, matching the paths declared in the package's post-install scripts.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n6. Before modifying any configuration, save a copy of each file you will edit and record a checksum of its original state, so you can prove the config was restored byte-for-byte. This is different from a backup for recovery purposes — here it is evidence for verification, and it should include a timestamp and the exact command that produced it. For example, before editing /etc/ssh/sshd_config to add a Match block, run cp -a /etc/ssh/sshd_config /root/config-evidence/sshd_config.orig and record sha256sum, then after rollback diff the live file against the saved copy.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n7. Enumerate every dependency the software pulls in and decide in advance which of those dependencies existed before and which are new, because rollback must not remove libraries that other software shares. A dependency that predates your install must survive your rollback untouched. For example, installing certbot on CentOS pulls in python3-certbot but python3-openssl is already present because the system's own tooling uses it, so your rollback plan should remove the former while explicitly excluding the latter.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n8. Verify dependency versions are pinned in your install record so that a rollback-and-reinstall test reproduces exactly the same dependency graph. Floating version references make reversibility unverifiable because a second install can silently pull different library versions. For example, in a Python project, a requirements.txt entry of requests==2.32.3 guarantees the reinstall after rollback installs the same version, whereas requests alone could resolve to a different release that week.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n9. Record file and directory permissions before installation and again after, then include the expected permission state in your rollback criteria, because mode bits that leak beyond the manifest will survive a package removal. Pay particular attention to world-writable files and setuid bits that a service might create at runtime. For example, after installing a build agent that writes to /var/cache/buildagent, verify that its directory is mode 0750 owned by the buildagent group, and confirm that rollback removes the directory entirely rather than leaving mode-0777 cache files behind.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n10. Verify that the software runs under a dedicated service account created at install time, and that the account itself is part of the rollback manifest. A dedicated user isolates the software's file ownership so that you can confidently remove all files owned by that uid after uninstall. For example, installing Grafana creates the grafana system user with uid 472, and a verified rollback should run userdel grafana and then confirm with find / -user grafana that no orphaned files remain on the filesystem.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n11. Test the service restart cycle explicitly, verifying that the software stops cleanly, its process disappears, and its ports are released, because a rollback that leaves a zombie holding a port will poison the reinstall test. Watch for daemons that respawn via socket activation or systemd timers even after you think you stopped them. For example, after installing a custom app served by apache2, run systemctl stop apache2 and verify with ss -tlnp that port 443 no longer appears, and check that no apache2 worker processes remain in ps.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n12. Verify that the software survives a full host reboot and that its startup mechanism is contained to a single identifiable unit file, so that rollback has one clean thing to disable. Startup hooks scattered across cron, rc.local, and shell profiles are nearly impossible to fully reverse. For example, installing a monitoring daemon that installs both a systemd unit /etc/systemd/system/monitor.service and an @reboot cron entry for the same user means your rollback must remove both, which you only discover by rebooting the host and re-checking with systemctl list-unit-files and crontab -l.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n13. Test interruption during installation in a disposable environment, because reversibility means the system must also survive a half-finished install. The safest test is a VM snapshot, a partial install, and an attempt to remove the package while interrupted. For example, in a throwaway VirtualBox VM, start apt install postgresql-16 and kill the process mid-way through the post-install script, then verify that dpkg --configure -a repairs state and apt remove --purge postgresql-16 returns the system to the baseline snapshot.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n14. Simulate a network interruption during installation, since downloads are the most common failure point and a failed fetch can leave partially unpacked archives in caches or temp directories. The test should confirm that a retry either completes cleanly or the abort path deletes the partial artifacts. For example, install Ansible via pip inside a Docker container and use the network simulation tool trickle to throttle and then cut the download of the ansible-core wheel, verifying that pip's cache directory contains no half-written wheel that a subsequent install would corrupt.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n15. Take a system-level backup immediately before installation, in a form that you can prove you can actually restore from, because a backup you have not tested is only a hope. The restore must be exercised on a separate machine or VM so that testing it does not destroy the evidence. For example, before installing Docker Engine on a production-adjacent VM, run LVM's lvcreate --snapshot to create a 2 GB snapshot, then restore the snapshot onto a second cloned disk in a spare VM to prove that it boots with the pre-Docker package list.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n16. Back up the configuration directory separately from the system snapshot, because configuration rollback often needs finer granularity than a whole-disk restore. A per-file backup with checksums lets you restore a single config file while keeping other intentional changes intact. For example, before editing nginx's site config to add a reverse proxy block for the new app, copy /etc/nginx/sites-available/default to a timestamped tar archive in /var/backups/installs/ with a sha256 manifest, so that rollback can restore just that file via tar --extract without overwriting an unrelated TLS change made that week.\n\n3 sentences.\n\n17. Verify that the installation separates program files from user data by design, because reversibility breaks down the moment rollback has to decide whether a file is the application's or the user's. Check the install manifest against the standard hierarchy to ensure data lives in /var/lib or the documented data dir, not next to binaries. For example, when installing Gitea from its release package, confirm that custom configuration lives in /var/lib/gitea/custom and repositories in /var/lib/gitea/data, while binaries are in /usr/local/bin, so that a rollback that removes only the manifest files provably leaves the 