{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-942e081bbb1e4762","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nREADY\n\n1. Verification begins by defining reversibility as a measurable contract before you touch the system, because an unreversible claim is just a guess until it is testable. Write down the exact end state that must be restored, including file paths, service states, and configuration values that must be identical before and after. For example, if you install nginx on Ubuntu 24.04, your contract states that after rollback the /etc/nginx directory must be absent, the www-data service listener on port 80 must be gone, and no nginx processes may remain in the process table.\n\n2. Take a machine-readable baseline of the system before installing anything, so that you have a ground truth to compare against later rather than relying on memory. The baseline should capture installed packages, listening ports, running services, and checksums of configuration directories the software is likely to touch. For example, on a Fedora server, run `rpm -qa > baseline-packages.txt`, `ss -tlnp > baseline-ports.txt`, and `sha256sum /etc/hosts` to create snapshot files you can diff after rollback.\n\n3. Verify the installation method itself is reversible by choosing a transactional package manager over a manual tarball extraction whenever possible. A package manager records the manifest of every file it writes, giving you an authoritative list to remove later, whereas a tarball leaves no such record. For example, installing htop with `apt install htop` records every path in dpkg's database, while extracting a htop tarball into /usr/local scatters files you must remember and find yourself.\n\n4. Confirm after installation that the package manager's recorded manifest matches what actually landed on disk, because partial installs silently break rollback assumptions. Compare the list of files the package claims to own against a filesystem diff built from your pre-install snapshot. For example, after installing redis-server on Debian, run `dpkg -L redis-server` and diff that list against the delta from a find-based snapshot, verifying nothing appeared in /etc/redis beyond the documented redis.conf.\n\n5. Verify that configuration files are written to predictable, documented locations rather than scattered across the system, because scattered configs are the most common source of rollback residue. Check that the software respects standard hierarchy paths, keeping system config in /etc and internal state in its designated data directory. For example, when installing Prometheus, confirm the main config sits in /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml and the TSDB lives in /var/lib/prometheus, exactly matching the paths declared in the package's post-install scripts.\n\n6. Before modifying any configuration, save a copy of each file you will edit along with a checksum of its original state, so you can later prove it was restored byte-for-byte. This differs from an operational backup: here it is verification evidence, and it should record the timestamp and the exact command that produced it. For example, before editing /etc/ssh/sshd_config to add a Match block, run `cp -a` into /root/config-evidence/sshd_config.orig with `sha256sum` output, then after rollback diff the live file against the stored copy.\n\n7. Enumerate every dependency the software pulls in and decide in advance which existed before your install and which are new, because rollback must not remove shared libraries that other software still needs. A dependency that predates your installation must survive your rollback untouched. For example, installing certbot on CentOS pulls python3-certbot as new while python3-openssl was already present for system tooling, so your rollback plan removes the former and explicitly protects the latter.\n\n8. Verify that dependency versions are pinned in your install record, so that a rollback-and-reinstall test reproduces exactly th