 example, in a throwaway VirtualBox VM, start `apt install postgresql-16`, kill the process mid-post-install-script, then verify that `dpkg --configure -a` repairs the package state and `apt remove --purge postgresql-16` returns the system to baseline.\n\n14. Simulate a network interruption during installation, since downloads are the most common failure point and a cut fetch can leave partially unpacked archives in caches and temporary directories. The test should confirm that either the retry completes cleanly or the abort path deletes the partial artifacts. For example, install Ansible via pip inside a Docker container and use trickle to throttle then sever the download of the ansible-core wheel, verifying that pip's cache holds no half-written wheel that a later install would corrupt.\n\n15. Take a system-level backup immediately before installation, in a form you have actually proven restorable, because an untested backup is a hope, not a guarantee. Exercise the restore on a separate machine or VM so that testing it does not destroy your live evidence. For example, before installing Docker Engine on a production-adjacent VM, create an LVM snapshot with `lvcreate --snapshot`, then restore that snapshot onto a second cloned disk in a spare VM to prove it boots with the pre-Docker package list.\n\n16. Back up configuration directories separately from the full system snapshot, because config rollback often needs finer granularity than a whole-disk restore can offer. A per-file backup with a checksum manifest lets you restore one config file without clobbering other deliberate changes. For example, before editing an nginx sites-available file to add a reverse proxy block for the new app, copy it to a timestamped tar archive under /var/backups/installs/ with a sha256 manifest, so rollback restores just that file via `tar --extract` without touching an unrelated TLS change made that week.\n\n17. Verify that the installation separates program files from user data by design, because reversibility breaks down the moment rollback must decide whether a file belongs to the application or the user. Check the install manifest against the filesystem hierarchy to ensure data lives in /var/lib or the documented data directory, never beside binaries. For example, when installing Gitea from its release package, confirm configuration lives in /var/lib/gitea/custom and repositories in /var/lib/gitea/data while the binary sits in /usr/local/bin, so a rollback that removes only manifest files provably leaves user repositories untouched.\n\n18. Prove with a live test that the installation does not modify user data outside its declared footprint, by seeding canary files and verifying they are unchanged. Create a marker file in a home directory and a dummy database row, then check byte-identity after the install-configure-rollback cycle. For example, before installing the Nextcloud server package, create ~/canary.txt containing the string leopard-42 with a recorded checksum, and after the full cycle confirm the file still reads leopard-42 with an unchanged modification time, proving the installer never wandered into the home directory.\n\n19. Verify the rollback through the same mechanism used for installation first, because the package manager's own removal path is the shortest and most auditable reversal. A purge removal is stronger than a plain removal, since it also deletes configuration files the package created. For example, rolling back an installed jq via `apt purge jq` passes verification only if `dpkg -l jq` shows the package as deinstalled with no config-files row and `which jq` returns nothing.\n\n20. Verify snapshot-level rollback as a second, independent path, because some installations modify state outside the package database and only an image restore catches those. Test the restore in the same virtual machine by installing over the snapshot and then reverting the disk. For example, on a Proxmox host, snapshot VM 101 before installing fail2ban, install it, revert the snapshot, and verify that `apt list --installed` no longer mentions fail2ban and /etc/fail2ban is gone, proving the hypervisor-level safety net works.\n\n21. After rollback, hunt for leftover files that neither the package manager nor the snapshot caught by diffing the post-rollback filesystem against the pre-install baseline. Common residue includes logs, runtime directories, and caches created on first run rather than at install time. For example, after removing the filebeat shipper, a diff between baseline and current find output reveals /var/lib/filebeat/registry still exists because the running agent created it after installation, so the rollback procedure must be amended to delete that path explicitly.\n\n22. Verify that service state and integration points are restored after rollback, including disabled units, removed firewall rules, and unregistered cron jobs. Package files can be gone while their integrations persist and silently expose or destabilize the system. For example, after rolling back a VPN client install that had added a ufw rule allowing UDP 51820, verify with `ufw status numbered` that the rule is absent, and confirm `systemctl is-enabled` on the client's unit returns a not-found error rather than enabled.\n\n23. Run the entire cycle end-to-end once in a clean test environment — baseline, install, configure, restart, rollback, diff — and accept nothing less than an empty diff against the baseline as passing. A full-cycle run matters because individual reversibility checks can each pass while their combination leaks something. For example, on a Vagrant machine defined by a fresh Vagrantfile, script the cycle and run it three times consecutively, confirming the post-rollback diff against baseline is empty every time, proving the procedure does not depend on leftover state from a previous run.\n\n24. Document the verified procedure as an executable runbook, so reversibility becomes a repeatable property of the system rather than the memory of one person. The runbook should contain the exact baseline commands, install commands, verification diffs, and rollback commands, each annotated with the output that counts as passing. For example, commit a rollback.sh into the deployment repository that recreates the checksum baseline, installs vault from HashiCorp's apt repository, and after removal asserts vault is absent, then run it in CI on every release so the guarantee is continuously re-verified instead of assumed.","ttft_ms":117953,"type":"result","duration_ms":118232,"uuid":"9ad356b5-3aad-4073-8b04-f515fa06c4a6","ttft_stream_ms":117620,"time_to_request_ms":112,"first_content_frame_ms":117625,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
